rules_version = '2';

// ContractNest — Firebase Storage security rules
// ============================================================================
// DENY ALL DIRECT CLIENT ACCESS. Firebase is a dumb blob store: it holds bytes
// and nothing else. Every read and write is brokered by the API with the Admin
// SDK, which authorises against contract_membership() in Postgres and then
// mints a short-TTL signed URL.
//
// Signed URLs are NOT governed by these rules — they carry their own signature —
// so denying everything here closes the client SDK door without closing the
// broker's.
//
// Why the rule lives here and the permission lives in Postgres: a service
// contract is shared between buyer and seller, and its proofs belong to the
// contract, not to either party's namespace. Expressing that in Firebase rules
// would mean a second copy of the access model, in a second language, that
// drifts from the first silently. So there is only one copy, and it sits beside
// the contract row that defines it.
//
// ⚠️ DO NOT DEPLOY THIS UNTIL BATCH D IS LIVE.
// The API today still uploads through the Firebase CLIENT SDK with
// signInAnonymously() in three places (storageService, tenantProfileService,
// integrationService). Those calls ARE subject to these rules, so deploying
// deny-all before their call sites are migrated to the broker would break
// avatar, logo, block-icon and QR uploads immediately.
//
// Deploy with:  firebase deploy --only storage
// Verify with:  a client-SDK read from a test harness must FAIL, and an
//               API-minted signed URL must still work.

service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
